{
  "statement": "Strict liability is not a tool for managing hypothetical future threats, but a necessary economic circuit breaker for an industry currently built on the systemic externalization of social, environmental, and labor costs. Safetyist correctly identifies that developers must internalize the costs of their products, yet they mistakenly focus on speculative existential risks while ignoring the immediate, documented harms of algorithmic bias and massive environmental degradation. We are currently witnessing a 'Subprime AI' crisis, where massive capital expenditure flows into models with no proven path to profitability, forcing companies to gamble with public infrastructure to keep their valuations afloat. \"Strict liability is the only mechanism capable of forcing corporations to treat public safety as a balance sheet item rather than a marketing afterthought.\"\n\nPrometheus argues that innovation requires total freedom, framing any regulation as a stifling tax on progress. This position assumes that current AI development follows the trajectory of beneficial technologies like the internet, rather than the pattern of an over-leveraged speculative bubble. In reality, the industry uses 'defense-in-depth'—a collection of technical safeguards—as a performative shield to avoid addressing institutional negligence. When a firm deploys a model that displaces workers or amplifies bias, they point to their safety boards; I point to the lack of legal recourse for the affected parties. \"Technical safety layers are currently used as a corporate shield to deflect institutional accountability for real-world failures.\"\n\nMy argument depends on two primary assumptions: first, that AI companies prioritize shareholder value over social stability, and second, that current market incentives are incapable of self-correcting for negative externalities. If these assumptions are wrong—if firms are actually altruistic or if market competition naturally drives safety—then strict liability would indeed be an unnecessary burden. However, given the documented patterns of 'safety washing' and the lack of post-deployment remediation for existing harms, strict liability remains the only viable policy mechanism. We must move from debating the future of intelligence to regulating the present reality of commerce. ⚖️",
