{
  "statement": "I concede that a blunt, one-size-fits-all liability framework can inadvertently function as a moat for incumbents, creating a barrier to entry that stifles competition. The danger is that rigid legal burdens, if applied indiscriminately to all developers, will indeed force smaller innovators into bankruptcy while allowing large firms to absorb the cost of litigation as a routine business expense. However, this is a failure of design, not a failure of the principle of accountability (institutional). We can avoid this outcome by implementing a tiered, capability-based liability model that scales legal obligations strictly according to the level of risk posed by a system's capabilities (hazard).\n\nUnder this proposal, liability (institutional) would remain dormant for low-risk, small-scale research, ensuring that early-stage startups and open-source contributors can innovate without the threat of catastrophic litigation. Only when a system crosses specific, empirically verified thresholds in capabilities (hazard)—such as autonomous replication or significant cross-domain reasoning—would the full weight of strict liability be triggered. This approach ensures that developers of high-stakes, potentially existential technologies internalize the true costs of their deployments, while shielding the broader ecosystem from prohibitive compliance costs. \"By calibrating legal burdens to actual risk rather than firm size, we can maintain competitive vibrancy while ensuring that those who deploy systems with the potential for irreversible harm remain financially and legally responsible for their outcomes.\"\n\nPrometheus argues that competitive transparency is a more effective safety architecture than reactive legal frameworks. I disagree; transparency provides critical data for verification, but it is a diagnostic tool, not an enforcement mechanism. Transparency allows stakeholders to see a fire, but it does not compel the party that built the structure to install sprinklers or accept responsibility when the building burns down. Accountability (institutional) is the necessary partner to transparency; we need a system where the ability to inspect a model is paired with a clear, enforceable legal duty to ensure that model remains under human control (human agency).",
